  <h2>How the Family Sharing Feature Works</h2>
  <p>
    YumePad's family sharing architecture is designed with several principles that distinguish it from simpler alert-based systems:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Consent and control by the monitored person.</strong> The elderly user controls which family members receive sleep summaries, what level of detail is shared, and can revoke access at any time. The system is explicitly designed so that the monitored person is always the data owner and the sharing is always at their discretion. This design choice is not merely ethical — it is practical: elderly users who feel in control of their health data are significantly more likely to use the system consistently and to engage positively with their own sleep information.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Summary rather than live feed.</strong> Family members do not receive a live data stream from the parent's bedroom. They receive a once-daily sleep summary, delivered at a configurable time (typically morning), that shows the previous night's sleep quality overview: sleep duration, deep sleep percentage, heart rate range, any bed-exit events, and a simple overall assessment. This summary format respects the temporal boundary between observation and action — the family learns about the night in the morning, not moment-by-moment throughout it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Alert thresholds configured by medical history.</strong> Families can configure alert conditions — extended bed absence, vital sign excursion beyond personal baseline, unusually fragmented sleep — that trigger an immediate notification rather than waiting for the morning summary. Alert thresholds are adjustable and can be set conservatively for elderly users with specific health conditions (post-cardiac event, recent fall history, known dementia) or loosely for users whose primary monitoring goal is general reassurance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Integration with existing care team.</strong> YumePad's export function allows the monitored user or designated family member to share a monthly sleep trend report with a physician, facilitating the kind of longitudinal health conversation that appointment-based medicine rarely accommodates.
  </p>

  <h2>The Night That Changed Everything</h2>
  <p>
    For many YumePad families, there is a specific night — a night when the system's alert triggered at 4 AM because the elderly user had been out of bed for forty-five minutes, and the adult child who called found their parent confused and unable to stand — that crystallizes the value of the system in a way that months of normal nights cannot.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the deeper value is precisely the opposite: the accumulation of normal nights, each morning's quiet confirmation that last night was fine, the slow building of a trust relationship with the data that allows the distant caregiver to sleep through the night themselves. The goal of good elder monitoring technology is not to manufacture emergencies — it is to manage the anxiety that lives in the space where information is absent, so that the caregiver's own health and sleep are not consumed by it.
  </p>

